 "sourceName": "Theodosius II", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "abolition-of-olympic-games", "targetName": "Abolition of Olympic Games", "context": "The decrees of Emperor Theodosius II in 426 CE, which abolished pagan festivals, solidified the historical significance of Aurelios Zopyros's victory as one of the last."}], "places": [{"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Olympia, Greece", "role": "Site of Olympic victory"}, {"name": "Roman Empire", "role": "Broader political context"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Aurelios Zopyros", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "aurelios-zopyros"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q562856", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aurelios_Zopyros"], "tags": ["Athletes & Competitors", "cause-and-effect", "cultural-diffusion", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Aurelios Zopyros's victory in 400 CE as the last known Olympic boxing champion serves as a poignant marker for the decline and eventual abolition of the ancient Olympic Games, symbolizing the broader cultural shift from paganism to Christianity in the Late Roman Empire.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-27T01:57:23Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40432858017", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Aurelios Zopyros (b. 400 CE, Athens) was a athlete or sports figure of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in Athens. Known in the fields of athletes & competitors.", "newValue": "Aurelios Zopyros, born in Athens around 400 CE, holds a unique and poignant place in the annals of athletic history as the last known boxing champion of the ancient Olympic Games. His victory at the 291st Olympiad, occurring amidst the twilight of pagan traditions and the ascendance of Christianity within the Roman Empire, symbolically marks the end of an era stretching back nearly twelve centuries. Zopyros's triumph, though a personal achievement, is largely remembered for its historical positioning, serving as a terminal point in the long and storied tradition of the quadrennial athletic festival.

His victory in the *pygmachia*, or boxing, event of the 291st Olympic Games in 400 CE, occurred in Olympia, the sacred site where the games had been held since at least 776 BCE. By this point, the Olympic Games had significantly diminished in prestige and scale compared to their classical zenith. The Roman Empire, now predominantly Christian, viewed such pagan festivals with increasing hostility. While the precise details of Zopyros's boxing style or opponents are lost to history, his name is preserved in a handful of historical records, primarily due to the chronological significance of his win as the final recorded boxing victor.

Zopyros's legacy is not merely that of an athlete, but as a symbolic figure marking the definitive shift from the classical world's veneration of physical prowess and pagan ritual to the Christianized Late Antiquity. His victory preceded the official prohibition of all pagan cults and festivals by Emperor Theodosius II in 426 CE, which included the Olympic Games. Thus, Aurelios Zopyros stands as a final, fleeting echo of a deeply entrenched cultural institution, a tangible link between the Hellenic past and its eventual dissolution."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-27T01:57:23Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40432858017", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "400 CE, Athens"}, {"title": "Development of competitive athletics and sporting traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "400 CE, Athens"}], "newValue": ["The enduring tradition of the ancient Olympic Games provided the stage for Aurelios Zopyros's athletic career.", "The cultural importance of athletic prowess and competitive festivals in Classical Greek society sustained the games for centuries.", "The continued, albeit declining, support for pagan festivals into the late Roman Empire allowed the 291st Olympiad to take place."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-27T01:57:23Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40432858017", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Inspired sporting achievement and athletic culture", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "Classical"}], "newValue": ["Aurelios Zopyros's victory marked the conclusive end of the boxing tradition within the ancient Olympic Games.", "He became a symbolic figure representing the narrative of the decline and eventual abolition of ancient pagan festivals.", "His name is pres